# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Inhale Occupies Beat Three

**Story & Context:** The debut begins at 160 BPM with a short serpent phrase lasting exactly one and a half bars. Noor must inhale on beat three of the second bar. Darius leaves that first inhale completely empty, making the missing low sound feel larger than a drum hit. On the next cycle, he fills the same breath slot with a three-kick footwork cluster. Mara places one muted clavichord fifth immediately after Noor re-enters, giving the bent serpent note a fixed reference. Kojo answers only every fourth phrase with a rising talking-drum cell.

As the piece develops, the inhale remains in the same physiological place but receives different treatment each cycle: silence, kick cluster, vocal count slice, clavichord knock, then silence again. The listener begins anticipating Noor’s need for air as strongly as the snare pattern. In the final section, Darius removes the entire beat during the inhale and lets Noor’s audible breath become the loudest transient in the bar. The track ends on the next inhalation before the serpent can return.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close view of a velodrome lap chart with beat three circled in pencil, serpent mouthpiece and breath condensation near a microphone, compact sampler pads and a tiny clavichord key visible at frame edge. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “beat three: breathe,” “leave it open,” “fill the next one,” and “air first.”

## Track 2: Sub Arrives After the Leather Buzz

**Story & Context:** The second track demonstrates the Late-Sub Shadow rule. Noor plays a low serpent fundamental with a rough mouthpiece buzz. Darius delays the sine sub by a fraction of a beat so the acoustic attack is unmistakable before electronic weight appears underneath it. The sub exits early, leaving the serpent body resonance exposed at the end of every note.

Mara builds the harmony from two prepared-clavichord dyads separated by a minor second. Kojo uses downward pressure bends to answer Noor’s upward lip inflections. The drums become progressively denser, but each serpent phrase gets shorter to prevent fatigue. At the apparent climax, Noor plays only single low notes surrounded by rapid machine detail. The largest sound in the mix is therefore also the least frequent.

The final sixteen bars remove sub completely. Noor repeats the opening fundamental alone while Darius preserves the same drum density, proving that the track’s low-frequency identity comes from breath and mouthpiece vibration rather than synthesized bass.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Extreme close-up of a serpent mouthpiece wrapped in dark leather beside a subwoofer cone, a waveform diagram showing the acoustic attack beginning before the electronic sine wave, prepared clavichord strings behind. Spoken fragments limited to “buzz first,” “sub later,” “leave before release,” and “air owns the low end.”

## Track 3: Lap Counter Reaches Zero Before the Lung Does

**Story & Context:** The EP closer starts with a sixteen-bar loop displayed on an old mechanical lap counter. Darius programs the most fragmented footwork pattern on the record, while Noor deliberately ignores the visible count and plays phrases according to comfortable breath length. Her final phrase crosses the projected zero and continues three beats beyond it.

Mara contributes isolated clavichord octaves exactly at the numerical boundaries, creating a rigid machine-time reference. Kojo does the opposite, placing talking-drum bends only when Noor changes breath direction. Two systems become audible at once: measured laps and human respiration.

During the final passage, the lap display counts down 4, 3, 2, 1, 0 while Darius strips the drums to rimshots and one dry kick. Noor still has air and sustains a rough low tone beyond zero. No one cuts her off. When she finally releases, Kojo plays one upward drum bend and Mara closes the track with a single dry clavichord note. The ending establishes the band’s principle: the machine may count time, but the body decides when the phrase is actually finished.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Mechanical cycling lap counter showing zero beneath empty velodrome seating, serpent player still exhaling into a microphone while the sampler display has stopped, one clavichord key depressed and talking drum in shadow. Spoken fragments limited to “zero is not the end,” “still breathing,” “let the counter stop,” and “finish when the lung does.”

